module nupp.io.uri

--[[
`nupp.io.uri` parses one absolute URI into an immutable value and answers its
components from the parse rather than from a substring. Reach for it when a
program routes, rewrites or inspects a resource identifier.

```nupp
const uri = nupp.io.uri

local endpoint, reason = uri.newURI("https://user@example.com:8443/api?q=1#top")
assert(endpoint, reason)
assert(endpoint:scheme() == "https")
assert(endpoint:host() == "example.com")
assert(endpoint:port() == 8443)
```

Two URIs are equal when their normalized text is, so a host or scheme written in
another case does not make one URI unequal to an otherwise identical one. Nothing has to be closed.

`URI` has no public constructor. Creation goes through `uri.newURI`, which interns
the 1024 most recently used normalized URIs. Repeated parsing and URI-producing
operations therefore reuse the same immutable value while retention stays
bounded.

## Reading components

`scheme` and `path` answer a string, and `path` is empty rather than nil when
the URI names none. `username` is empty when there is no user information.
Every other component is optional and answers nil when the URI does not write
it:

```nupp
const uri = nupp.io.uri

local plain = assert(uri.newURI("mailto:someone@example.com"))
assert(plain:host() == nil)
assert(plain:port() == nil)
assert(plain:path() == "someone@example.com")
```

The remaining accessors are `authority`, `password`, `userInfo`, `query` and
`fragment`. `toString` answers the normalized complete URI, and `tostring(uri)`
is the same call.

## Validating untrusted text

`uri.newURI` answers nil and a reason for malformed input, because bad text is an
ordinary answer. `uri.validate` asks the same question without retaining an
object, and `uri.isURI` separates a URI from a string or a record:

```nupp
const uri = nupp.io.uri

local ok, why = uri.validate("http://[")
assert(not ok and why)
assert(not uri.isURI("https://example.com"))
```

::: deepdive Parsing answers a reason, modification raises
A parse takes text from somewhere else, so failure is data and the caller is
handed it. A `with` operation starts from a URI that already parsed, so a
failure means the caller asked for something the grammar cannot express, which
is a mistake at the call site rather than a bad input. Text that came from
outside the program therefore goes through `uri.newURI`, `uri.validate` or
`resolve`, all of which answer a reason.
:::

## Deriving a new URI

Every `with` operation answers a new URI and leaves the original unchanged.
Supplying a component equal to its current normalized value answers the
receiver itself rather than reparsing:

```nupp
const uri = nupp.io.uri

local endpoint = assert(uri.newURI("https://user:pass@example.com:8443/api?q=1"))
local production = endpoint:withUserInfo(nil):withHost("api.example.com")
assert(production:userInfo() == nil)
assert(production:host() == "api.example.com")
assert(endpoint:host() == "example.com")
```

The operations are `withScheme`, `withUserInfo`, `withHost`, `withPort`,
`withPath`, `withQuery` and `withFragment`. Passing nil removes an optional
component. A port must be an integer from 0 through 65535, and anything else
raises.

`concatPath` appends path text without interpreting it as a reference:

```nupp
const uri = nupp.io.uri

local api = assert(uri.newURI("https://api.example.com/v1"))
assert(api:concatPath("users"):path() == "/v1/users")
```

`withEndpoint(endpoint)` takes another URI's scheme and authority and keeps the
receiver's path, query and fragment, which is what reroutes a request URI
through a configured service address.

## Resolving a reference

`resolve` applies RFC reference resolution, the way a browser resolves a link
against the page it is on. It answers nil and a reason when the reference
cannot be resolved:

```nupp
const uri = nupp.io.uri

local page = assert(uri.newURI("https://example.com/docs/guide/index.html"))
local image, reason = page:resolve("../images/avatar.png")
assert(image, reason)
assert(image:path() == "/docs/images/avatar.png")
```

## Building from components

`uri.newURI` also accepts a `nupp.io.uri.Components` record. The components are
assembled into text and then parsed, so one grammar decides what is valid:

```nupp
const uri = nupp.io.uri

local status = assert(uri.newURI({
    scheme = "https",
    userInfo = "reader:secret",
    host = "example.com",
    path = "/status",
    query = "full=1",
}))
assert(status:userInfo() == "reader:secret")
```

`scheme` is required and may not be empty. `userInfo`, `host`, `path`, `query`
and `fragment` are optional strings, and `port` is an optional integer from 0
through 65535. Every field is URI text rather than a filesystem name.

::: seealso
- [](nupp.io.path) for filesystem names, where the platform's separators and
  `..` rules apply instead
- [standard-library.md](docs/learn/runtime/data/standard-library.md) for how reaching a
  module selects the provider behind it
:::
]]

-- RFC 3986 section 5.2.4, so a resolved reference answers the path a browser
-- would navigate to rather than one still carrying "." and ".." segments.
const pathtext = require("nupp.io.uri.pathtext")
const provider = require("nupp.io.uri.provider")

--- The parts a URI is built from when it is not written out as text.
--- @export
export record Components
    --- The scheme, which is required and may not be empty.
    scheme: string

    --- The user information before `@`, when there is any.
    userInfo: string?

    --- The host, when there is an authority.
    host: string?

    --- The port, 0 through 65535.
    port: integer?

    --- The path, which may be empty.
    path: string?

    --- The query after `?`, without the `?`.
    query: string?

    --- The fragment after `#`, without the `#`.
    fragment: string?
end

--- A parsed URI.
--- @export
export record URI
    private _text: string
    private _scheme: string
    private _authority: string?
    private _username: string
    private _password: string?
    private _host: string?
    private _port: integer?
    private _path: string
    private _query: string?
    private _fragment: string?

    --- The whole URI as text.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @return the URI text
    function toString(self): string
        return self._text
    end

    --- The scheme, without the colon.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @return the scheme
    function scheme(self): string
        return self._scheme
    end

    --- The authority between `//` and the path.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @return the authority, or nil when there is none
    function authority(self): string?
        return self._authority
    end

    --- The user name before any password.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @return the user name, empty when there is none
    function username(self): string
        return self._username
    end

    --- The password after the user name.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @return the password, or nil when there is none
    function password(self): string?
        return self._password
    end

    --- The host.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @return the host, or nil when there is no authority
    function host(self): string?
        return self._host
    end

    --- The port, when one was written.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @return the port, or nil when the URI names none
    function port(self): integer?
        return self._port
    end

    --- The path, which is empty rather than nil when absent.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @return the path
    function path(self): string
        return self._path
    end

    --- The query, without the `?`.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @return the query, or nil when there is none
    function query(self): string?
        return self._query
    end

    --- The fragment, without the `#`.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @return the fragment, or nil when there is none
    function fragment(self): string?
        return self._fragment
    end

    --- The user information as written, user name and password together.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @return the user information, or nil when there is none
    function userInfo(self): string?
        if self._username == "" and self._password == nil then
            return nil
        end
        return self._username .. (self._password ~= nil and ":" .. self._password or "")
    end

    --- The URI with its scheme replaced.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @param value the scheme to use, without the colon
    --- @return the new URI, or this one when nothing changes
    --- @raises when the scheme cannot be used here
    function withScheme(self, value: string): URI
        return assert(newURI(value .. self._text:sub(#self._scheme + 1)))
    end

    --- The URI with its user information replaced.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @param value the user information, or nil to remove it
    --- @return the new URI, or this one when nothing changes
    --- @raises when the user information cannot be used here
    function withUserInfo(self, value: string?): URI
        return assert(
            newURI(
                new Components(
                    scheme = self._scheme,
                    userInfo = value,
                    host = self._host,
                    port = self._port,
                    path = self._path,
                    query = self._query,
                    fragment = self._fragment
                )
            )
        )
    end

    --- The URI with its host replaced.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @param value the host, or nil to remove the authority
    --- @return the new URI, or this one when nothing changes
    --- @raises when the host cannot be used here
    function withHost(self, value: string?): URI
        return assert(
            newURI(
                new Components(
                    scheme = self._scheme,
                    userInfo = self:userInfo(),
                    host = value,
                    port = self._port,
                    path = self._path,
                    query = self._query,
                    fragment = self._fragment
                )
            )
        )
    end

    --- The URI with its port replaced.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @param value the port, 0 through 65535, or nil to remove it
    --- @return the new URI, or this one when nothing changes
    --- @raises when port is not a port number
    function withPort(self, value: integer?): URI
        return assert(
            newURI(
                new Components(
                    scheme = self._scheme,
                    userInfo = self:userInfo(),
                    host = self._host,
                    port = value,
                    path = self._path,
                    query = self._query,
                    fragment = self._fragment
                )
            )
        )
    end

    --- The URI with its path replaced.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @param value the path
    --- @return the new URI, or this one when nothing changes
    --- @raises when the path cannot be used here
    function withPath(self, value: string): URI
        if self._authority ~= nil and value ~= "" and value:sub(1, 1) ~= "/" then
            value = "/" .. value
        end
        return assert(
            newURI(
                new Components(
                    scheme = self._scheme,
                    userInfo = self:userInfo(),
                    host = self._host,
                    port = self._port,
                    path = value,
                    query = self._query,
                    fragment = self._fragment
                )
            )
        )
    end

    --- The URI with its query replaced.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @param value the query, without the `?`, or nil to remove it
    --- @return the new URI, or this one when nothing changes
    --- @raises when the query cannot be used here
    function withQuery(self, value: string?): URI
        return assert(
            newURI(
                new Components(
                    scheme = self._scheme,
                    userInfo = self:userInfo(),
                    host = self._host,
                    port = self._port,
                    path = self._path,
                    query = value,
                    fragment = self._fragment
                )
            )
        )
    end

    --- The URI with its fragment replaced.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @param value the fragment, without the `#`, or nil to remove it
    --- @return the new URI, or this one when nothing changes
    --- @raises when the fragment cannot be used here
    function withFragment(self, value: string?): URI
        return assert(
            newURI(
                new Components(
                    scheme = self._scheme,
                    userInfo = self:userInfo(),
                    host = self._host,
                    port = self._port,
                    path = self._path,
                    query = self._query,
                    fragment = value
                )
            )
        )
    end

    --- The URI with more appended to its path.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @param value the path to append
    --- @return the new URI, or this one when path is empty
    --- @raises when the path cannot be appended
    function concatPath(self, value: string): URI
        return self:withPath(self._path:gsub("/$", "") .. "/" .. value:gsub("^/", ""))
    end

    --- Resolves a reference against this URI, as a browser resolves a link.
    ---
    --- The reference decides how much of this URI survives: an absolute one
    --- replaces all of it, a rooted path keeps the scheme and authority, and a
    --- relative path is taken from this one's directory.
    ---
    --- #### Examples
    ---
    --- Resolve a relative reference:
    ---
    --- ```nupp
    --- local page = assert(newURI("https://example.com/docs/a.html"))
    --- assert(assert(page:resolve("b.html")):toString()
    ---     == "https://example.com/docs/b.html")
    --- ```
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @param reference the reference to resolve
    --- @return the resolved URI, or nil when the reference cannot be resolved
    --- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
    function resolve(self, reference: string): (URI?, string?)
        if reference:match("^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*:") then
            return newURI(reference)
        end
        local prefix = self._scheme .. ":"
        if reference:sub(1, 2) == "//" then
            -- A network-path reference supplies its own authority.
            return newURI(prefix .. reference)
        end
        local suffix = ""
        local beforeFragment, fragment = reference:match("^(.-)(#.*)$")
        if beforeFragment ~= nil then
            reference, suffix = beforeFragment, fragment
        end
        local beforeQuery, query = reference:match("^(.-)(%?.*)$")
        if beforeQuery ~= nil then
            reference, suffix = beforeQuery, query .. suffix
        elseif reference == "" then
            -- A same-document reference keeps the base's query.
            suffix = (self._query ~= nil and "?" .. self._query or "") .. suffix
        end
        local authority = self._authority ~= nil and "//" .. self._authority or ""
        local path: string
        if reference == "" then
            path = self._path
        elseif reference:sub(1, 1) == "/" then
            path = pathtext.removeDotSegments(reference)
        elseif self._authority ~= nil and self._path == "" then
            path = pathtext.removeDotSegments("/" .. reference)
        else
            path = pathtext.removeDotSegments((self._path:match("^(.*/)") or "") .. reference)
        end

        return newURI(prefix .. authority .. path .. suffix)
    end

    --- The URI with another's scheme and authority.
    --- @param self this URI
    --- @param endpoint the URI to take the endpoint from
    --- @return the new URI
    --- @raises when endpoint is not a URI
    function withEndpoint(self, endpoint: URI): URI
        return endpoint:concatPath(self._path):withQuery(self._query):withFragment(self._fragment)
    end

    metamethod __tostring: function(self): string
    metamethod __eq: function(self, any): boolean
end

    ;
(URI as {[string]: any}).__tostring = function(self: URI): string
    return self:toString()
end;
(URI as {[string]: any}).__eq = function(left: URI, right: URI): boolean
    return left:toString() == right:toString()
end

local function fromComponents(components: any): (string?, string?)
    if type(components) ~= "table" then
        return nil, "nupp: io.URI.newURI needs absolute text or URI components"
    end
    local scheme = components.scheme
    if type(scheme) ~= "string" or not (scheme as string):match("^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*$") then
        return nil, "nupp: URI components need a non-empty scheme"
    end
    for _, name in ipairs({"userInfo", "host", "path", "query", "fragment",}) do
        local value = components[name]
        if value ~= nil and type(value) ~= "string" then
            return nil, "nupp: URI component " .. name .. " must be a string or nil"
        end
    end
    local port = components.port
    if port ~= nil and (type(port) ~= "number" or port ~= math.floor(port) or port < 0 or port > 65535) then
        return nil, "nupp: URI component port must be an integer from 0 through 65535 or nil"
    end
    -- A delimiter that would end the component early. `#` always starts the
    -- fragment, `?` starts the query anywhere before one, and inside the
    -- authority `/`, `?` and `#` end it while `@` moves the host boundary.
    for name, delimiters in pairs({userInfo = "[/?#]", host = "[/?#@]", path = "[?#]", query = "#",}) do
        local value = components[name]
        if value ~= nil and (value as string):find(delimiters) ~= nil then
            return nil, "nupp: URI component " .. name .. " cannot contain a component delimiter"
        end
    end
    -- RFC 3986 section 3.3: beside an authority a path is empty or rooted, and
    -- without one it must not begin with `//`, which would reparse as one.
    local path = components.path
    if components.host or components.userInfo or port then
        if path ~= nil and path ~= "" and (path as string):sub(1, 1) ~= "/" then
            return nil, "nupp: URI component path must be empty or begin with \"/\" beside an authority"
        end
    elseif path ~= nil and (path as string):sub(1, 2) == "//" then
        return nil, "nupp: URI component path cannot begin with \"//\" when there is no authority"
    end
    local out = scheme .. ":"
    if components.host or components.userInfo or port then
        out = out .. "//"
        if components.userInfo then
            out = out .. components.userInfo .. "@"
        end
        out = out .. (components.host or "")
        if port then
            out = out .. ":" .. tostring(port)
        end
    end
    out = out .. (components.path or "")
    if components.query then
        out = out .. "?" .. components.query
    end
    if components.fragment then
        out = out .. "#" .. components.fragment
    end

    return out, nil
end

const URI_CACHE_CAPACITY = 1024
local uriCache: {[string]: any} = {}
local uriCacheSize = 0
local uriCacheNewest: any = nil
local uriCacheOldest: any = nil

local function adopt(value: URI): URI
    local text = value:toString()
    local found = uriCache[text]
    if found ~= nil then
        if found ~= uriCacheNewest then
            local older, newer = found.older, found.newer
            if older ~= nil then
                older.newer = newer
            else
                uriCacheOldest = newer
            end
            if newer ~= nil then
                newer.older = older
            end
            found.older = uriCacheNewest
            found.newer = nil
            uriCacheNewest.newer = found
            uriCacheNewest = found
        end

        return found.value
    end

    local entry = {value = value, older = uriCacheNewest, newer = nil}
    if uriCacheNewest ~= nil then
        uriCacheNewest.newer = entry
    else
        uriCacheOldest = entry
    end
    uriCacheNewest = entry
    uriCache[text] = entry
    uriCacheSize = uriCacheSize + 1
    if uriCacheSize > URI_CACHE_CAPACITY then
        local evicted = uriCacheOldest
        uriCacheOldest = evicted.newer
        uriCacheOldest.older = nil
        uriCache[evicted.value:toString()] = nil
        uriCacheSize = uriCacheSize - 1
    end

    return value
end

--- Parses absolute URI text, or assembles one from components.
---
--- Text has to be absolute: a relative reference is resolved against a URI that
--- already exists, through `URI:resolve`.
---
--- #### Examples
---
--- Read a URI's parts:
---
--- ```nupp
--- const uri = nupp.io.uri
---
--- local address = assert(uri.newURI("https://example.com/a?b=1"))
--- assert(address:host() == "example.com" and address:query() == "b=1")
--- ```
---
--- Assemble one from components rather than writing the text out:
---
--- ```nupp
--- local endpoint = assert(uri.newURI({
---     scheme = "https",
---     host = "example.com",
---     path = "/a",
---     query = "b=1",
--- }))
--- ```
--- @param value the URI text, or the components to assemble
--- @return the URI, or nil when it could not be parsed
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function newURI(value: string | Components): (URI?, string?)
    local text: string
    if type(value) == "string" then
        text = value as string
    else
        local composed, reason = fromComponents(value)
        if composed == nil then
            return nil, reason
        end
        text = composed
    end
    local cached = uriCache[text]
    if cached ~= nil then
        return adopt(cached.value)
    end
    local parts, reason = provider.parse(text)
    if parts == nil then
        return nil, reason
    end

    return adopt(
        setmetatable(
            {
                _text = parts.text,
                _scheme = parts.scheme,
                _authority = parts.authority,
                _username = parts.username,
                _password = parts.password,
                _host = parts.host,
                _port = parts.port,
                _path = parts.path,
                _query = parts.query,
                _fragment = parts.fragment,
            } as any,
            URI as any
        ) as URI
    )

end

--- Reports whether text parses as an absolute URI, without building one.
--- @param value the text to test
--- @return whether it parses
--- @return why it did not, when it did not
--- @export
export function validate(value: string): (boolean, string?)
    local parsed, reason = newURI(value)
    return parsed ~= nil, reason
end

--- Reports whether a value is a URI.
--- @param value the value to test
--- @return whether it is a URI
--- @export
export function isURI(value: any): boolean
    return type(value) == "table" and getmetatable(value) == URI
end
